# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
import json, re, pathlib

base = pathlib.Path(r"c:\Users\binya\Downloads\Shulchan Aruch\newtry\OC_001\output")

def he(rel, seif, marker):
    t = (base / rel).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    pat = rf"seif: {seif}\nmarker: {re.escape(marker)}\n\*\*\*\* HEBREW \*\*\*\*\n(.*?)\n\*\*\*\* ENGLISH"
    m = re.search(pat, t, re.DOTALL)
    return m.group(1) if m else ""

# Pre-built translations for shorter blocks + load long from external if needed
T = json.loads(pathlib.Path(r"c:\Users\binya\Downloads\Shulchan Aruch\batch_translations.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

T["422|baer-heitev/part-001.txt|2:א"] = "Hallel. And they do not recite it in a house of mourning, for it is said there: \"The dead do not praise Hashem\" — which would be like mocking the deceased; and see siman 131 s.k. 10 what I wrote there. Women are exempt from all of Hallel because it is a positive time-bound commandment — and Magen Avraham; and see Yad Aharon."

T["426|biur-halacha/part-001.txt|2:ה"] = "And one blesses standing — in the Gemara it states: Tanna Debei Rabbi Yishmael: were Israel to merit only to receive the countenance of their Father in heaven once a month, it would suffice; Abaye said, etc. And the intent in what he said to receive countenance is that through the moon and the rest of the heavenly host that constantly guard their task we see the greatness of the Holy One blessed be He. And we bless \"He who performs truth\" upon the Holy One blessed be He, who is a true God whose action is truth — meaning that the action itself is also truth, that it endures forever, as in what is said \"and He established them forever.\" And the principle of blessing is to the Holy One blessed be He alone, and one must not bless or pray to another, heaven forbid, except through the moon His might is seen — and this is receiving the countenance of their Father in heaven [Peri Megadim]. And I heard from someone a reason for what is practiced in many places after kiddush levanah to say \"we will praise\" — namely so they should not err, heaven forbid, in that we go out toward the moon and rejoice toward it, that there is some concern of a notion that we give honor to the moon; therefore we say \"we will praise\" and conclude \"for Hashem is the God in heaven above,\" etc. — there is no other; and what we go out for is only to see the might of the Holy One blessed be He, who illuminates at His will to all who come into the world, as in what is said \"lift up your eyes on high and see who created these.\" And it is in the Gemara: Abaye said, therefore one must say it standing; Marimar and Mar Zutra would lean on their servants and bless — see his explanation in the Tur and in Yad Rama to Rabbi Meir HaLevi to tractate Sanhedrin, that because of their old age and heaviness they could not stand and leaned on their servants' shoulders in order to sanctify standing — see there; and it implies from this that in any case leaning for this matter is like standing for one who cannot stand; and see in the chiddushim of Ra'ah in the name of Turei Zahav that it is forbidden to lean then on his staff — and this is when he could stand otherwise."

T["447|chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:ה"] = "Forbidden on Pesach — that is, to drink it; but it is permitted in benefit, all the more so that it is permitted to retain it; so is explained in the responsum of the Rashba siman 174 and in Tur Choshen Mishpat siman 113, who also wrote that everything permitted to retain is permitted to benefit from — see there."

T["459|chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|1:ו"] = "To send the matzot. And in the responsum of the Rashba siman 124 he wrote that the Rambam sent matzot to the furrier far more than fifteen houses away, and concluded; and in the end he refrained, not because of the furrier's distance but because he feared lest the furrier be full and his bread be delayed outside for the measure of a mil or more; but to be concerned about removal of the hand we did not hear — end of his words; and see what the Mechaber and the Rav wrote in seif 2 in the hagahah."

T["460|machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt|1:ב"] = "And so wrote the Rashba, etc.: guarding from the time of harvest, etc. — and this is according to the Rif above in siman 453 seif 4; and see there in Peri Chadash that even for blocking. For the Rif, matzah for the mitzvah requires guarding for the sake of the mitzvah from the time of harvest. In the name of the Rashba that even an Israelite on a gentile's dough — and the intent is that if he told him to make it for Pesach it does not help, for the gentile acts according to his own intent."

T["462|peri-megadim/part-001.txt|1:ב"] = "For — in Yoreh De'ah 126:6 (textual error: 126) — if there is kosher wine in the upper vessel and in the lower vessel, stam yayin is touched: when one pours, the upper remainder is forbidden; and if there is water in it, see as Magen Avraham wrote in letter 2 and Ateret Zekenim letter 2; and in Orach Chayyim 14 there, letter 7, regarding stam yayin of ours there is no need to add water (unless it certainly touched stam yayin); and Eshel Avraham there letter 11: a practical difference in adding water for ours, that it should be nullified in six parts water — see there; and siman 134:65. What Orach Chayyim wrote that se'or and honey disqualify wine — see Yoreh De'ah 123; and Shach letter 8: for ours specifically, if its taste changed it does not forbid in stam yayin — see there."

T["466|kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|4:_"] = "4) There. One should not chew, etc. — and this is specifically in chewing, for there is no concern of chametzing; and likewise in mere biting; and likewise in mice biting there is no concern of chametzing. Responsum of the Rashba siman 488: R' Abba said, R' Abba said, etc.; and see above siman 453 seif 3; and in our words there b'siyata diShmaya."

T["468|mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt|10:_"] = "(38) That one should not let blood — for on erev Shavuot it happened that the field whose name is Tavuach came out, and had Israel not accepted the Torah it would have been slaughtered for their flesh and blood; and the rabbis decreed on every erev Yom Tov because of erev Shavuot. And in Ashkenaz they call it \"scharfen\" or \"kepf zetzen\"; some permit except for Rosh HaShanah, which is the day of judgment; in any case on erev Shavuot one must be stringent. And on the last erev Yom Tov of Pesach everything is permitted, for it is not a festival in its own right. And all this is when one wishes to let blood for health; but for danger many have already ruled leniently, and \"He guards the simple\" is Hashem; and even on erev Shavuot the Avnei Nezer permitted when doctors ordered it for an illness involving danger; but otherwise not."

T["472|kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt|8:_"] = "8) There. Until it darkens — that is, tzeit hakochavim. Chayei Adam, general rule 131 in the laws of the order, letter 3; Ben Aharon, portion Tzav, letter 28; Mishna Berurah letter 5; and see below siman 562 seif 1."

T["467|turei-zahav/part-001.txt|4:_"] = "We are not concerned for an unspecified pile, etc. — so wrote the Rashba in the responsum that the Beit Yosef brought, and so: even those piles that we saw upon which rain fell — we may be lenient with a doubt that perhaps water entered only in the upper sheaves but not in the belly of the pile; and if you say it entered — perhaps it did not have time to chametz, for everything that did not come in water — therefore it is a double doubt for leniency — end of his words. From this too there is a contradiction to the Rama's ruling below, for here it is permitted even for eating."

# Shorter specialized blocks
T["467|biur-halacha/part-001.txt|14:א"] = "Split wheat — see Taz, who wrote that according to the hagahah above in seif 9, even if it did not split but only softened, this whole seif applies; and so is the opinion of other Acharonim [Bach, Or Sameach, Peri Chadash, article of Mordechai, Gra Zal, Chayei Adam — and not like Shulchan Gavoah who tends to say that in roasting and salting the Rama admits we require splitting; and so Chayei Adam for salting]; except that the Rama relied here on what he wrote in seif 9. And behold, in great loss — such as that he is poor and it is a large important piece, or it prevents joy of Yom Tov — it is implied from the Gra Zal that one may be lenient and rely on the Mechaber's view that peeling a bit suffices; and so Peri Chadash that one may dig deep and discard all around it and it is permitted in eating. And behold, all other Acharonim did not mention in practice this view; but if it softened and did not split, perhaps one may rely on this; and it is possible that the Gra Zal who is lenient in great loss is also specifically when softened; and see in the responsum of the Rama siman 28 — it is proven there that even when softened they practice forbidding that whole piece; and perhaps in great loss he too would agree to be lenient — requires study. And know further that in doubt whether it softened one must not be stringent at all [Acharonim]."

T["467|eshel-avraham/part-001.txt|2:ב"] = "But to eat them. The Taz and Peri Chadash challenged: behold, even for d'oraisa we rely on sefek sefeka, all the more so for d'rabbanan in any amount; see siman 453 seif 3. One must say it deals when there is not sixty against the prohibition — see there until he concludes forbidden to eat them since there is no loss in retaining them; if so, when he has no other grain it is permitted. Shach in Yoreh De'ah wrote the reason: according to Mar Ukva, even if they did not split they are forbidden, and it is sefek sefeka of a dispute, not of reality. According to Chayei Adam siman 7 it is more lenient and he agreed to the Taz — see there. From his words remember what I heard from the mouth of the famous gaon Av Beit Din R' Shaul Narol of Amsterdam, may he live long, some novelties that arose for him in the house of study of Rishon LeTziyon; he preached on what Magen Avraham brought siman 442 s.k. 1: the Tur wrote that every thing not forbidden in eating from the Torah is permitted to mix and retain; and so Semak; but Tosafos at the beginning of Pesachim wrote it is forbidden, for the rabbis did not distinguish, and proved so from the mishnah that se'or must be burned, and although it was stiff and there is no d'oraisa prohibition in eating it, nevertheless it must be burned d'rabbanan, etc.; and according to the Tur that mishnah of se'or must deal with Yom Tov, etc. — end of his words; and it is clear to every discerning eye that this answer is very strained; but in my humble opinion there is room to understand the Tur's method, and on the contrary Tosafos' words need explanation — for Peri Chadash from what R' Gidal said in the name of Rav: one who consecrates from six and above, even in Cordovanian wheat, there is no concern, etc. — see at length; end of his words."

T["467|magen-avraham/part-001.txt|11:א"] = "The entire dough. So wrote the Rashba siman 176: the wheat kernel did not touch in only one place but here and there through the rolling of the dough; and we say in such a case \"here it was found, here it was\" (see siman 462). And furthermore, the water in the dough conducts what emerges from that grain throughout the dough; they did not say peeling except dry in dry; and even in a moist place Rav compared it to the place of circumcision at the time of salting; but in a place of gravy they said the gravy conducts even something that does not exude; and likewise bread found in dough — bread is apt to crumble and crumbs mixed in the dough; besides that it itself can chametz others even though baked — except my mind is not inclined that a small thing should chametz the entire dough, etc. — see there; it is proven he holds it forbids even cold — see siman 462."

T["467|peri-megadim/part-001.txt|10:ד"] = "If they placed the rounds in a bowl and cut and poured gravy and found a softened wheat kernel — one may say to forbid the pouring; and all the more they are stringent when there was not the measure of a mil in hot food there is no measure. And even though already cooked in fat, perhaps it was not cooked well; and all the more if they cut the wheat and found white flour — behold we saw it was not cooked well; and see siman 463 in Magen Avraham letter 4. And in the introduction we challenged from Pesachim 39b: cooked chametzes and we do not establish it in fruit juice; afterward in water it does not chametz; and according to Rashi that stiff is fine; and responsum of the Ran siman 59 — one may say such a case is fruit juice and water; and one may say stiff is nullified in sixty; and it will be explained in letter 21 on this."

T["467|peri-megadim/part-001.txt|14:א"] = "Forbidden — Orach Chayyim, Magen Avraham letter 16, and Chayei Adam: from time to time we establish prohibition, but from place to place we do not establish prohibition. And in the Tur: an incident where they peeled, etc., and our Rabbi Ra'avan forbade — see there — and he did not mention splitting; and the Mechaber to his method in seif 9: specifically in splitting; if not split but softened, even in kli rishon in any amount the dish is permitted; and the explanation is before boiling, before they boiled the chicken (not cold, and from place to place we do not establish prohibition); and so one may reconcile the words of the Taz with Magen Avraham to distinguish between time and place; and see letters 16 and 17; and it will be explained there further. And the Taz wrote: one should not challenge — behold it is a double doubt (textual error in some editions): perhaps it fell now and perhaps it was digested in the chicken; one may say in a whole wheat kernel even if split it is not digested. And what he wrote \"perhaps it did not chametz\" is difficult, for in splitting the Mechaber wrote and it is certainly chametz for us — and so in letter 15. And it is possible likewise for us: even softened is forbidden in peeling — why is it a double doubt; and one may answer that doubt of lack of wisdom is not in the category of doubt; nevertheless in letter 15 he permitted selling for us when softened through double doubt as this; but in one doubt when softened he forbids benefit — not like Chayei Adam, as written in letter 14 from this."

# 466 chok-yaakov 4:א - full from he_blocks
T["466|chok-yaakov/part-001.txt|4:א"] = "Water fell on the flour. And similarly the Bach is stringent in siman 453 also regarding flour from which mice ate; but if they only bit the sack and there is room to doubt that perhaps they ate nothing of the flour — even if found during Pesach one may permit retaining it. And in the responsum of Maharam of Lublin siman 119 he is somewhat lenient regarding sacks gnawed by mice in time of distress — see there. Also the Taz permits to take around the bitten place and the rest he will sift and eat on Pesach even if dried — end of his words. And Magen Avraham wrote: and in my humble opinion regarding mice one must be more stringent, for these mice are wicked — they go throughout the sack and eat; and all the more when placed in barrels, for then it certainly goes throughout the flour; therefore the above geonim were stringent; and so in Mabit to be stringent for this reason — see there. And in my humble opinion it appears one need not be stringent at all regarding mice that ate flour, and to permit through sifting in every case, because the essential law of flour that mice ate was not mentioned at all in any early posek — only the Acharonim learned this law from wheat kernels that mice bit, for we establish as one who says mouse spittle chametzes, as explained above siman 453. And in my humble opinion: even though mouse spittle chametzes, nevertheless in their eating from flour there is no concern of chametzing at all; and clear proof to this from the mishnah in chapter 9 of Parah mishnah 3: all creeping things that drank water of purification do not disqualify, because no saliva emerges from their mouths except the weasel — R' Eliezer says also the mouse; and the halachah is not like R' Eliezer, as the Rambam, Rashi, and Ra'avad wrote; and so the Rambam explicitly chapter 9 laws of Parah law 12 that no moisture emerges from its mouth; and so Tosafos first chapter of Chullin. Behold that even liquids apt to receive moisture nevertheless do not disqualify; all the more flour where each crumb is separate from its fellow — there is no concern at all that it becomes moist from what it does not eat. And one should not compare to wheat kernels that are one mass and because of the strength of the bite we are concerned that some moisture remained from what remained of the kernel — not so in flour, where one may be lenient in every case; and this is clear. But my mind is not decisive against the great Acharonim, and in particular since they are stringent and I am lenient; nevertheless one may permit to tie the bitten place and permit the rest in every case through sifting, as the Taz ruled; and one who is stringent in this did not consider its thinness. And all this when one does not see any moistness in the bitten place; but if one sees any moistness in the bitten place, one may attribute it to a common matter — perhaps the mice urinated on the flour; and see below s.k. 14 whether there is concern of chametzing from urine, and it is as if moistened with water."

# 447 peri-megadim 1:ז
T["447|peri-megadim/part-001.txt|1:ז"] = "And after consideration I saw that what the Mechaber wrote in Yoreh De'ah 134:2 regarding small barrels being nullified — these are clear matters for halachah; and Pesach is different, for they made several stringencies from what is written: dry in dry is not nullified. And not as the Rashba wrote in Teshuvot HaRosh kvav and in responsum to the Mechaber, and also that we do not follow after the reason. And proof to this from what is written in Pesachim 43a: Rava said the halachah is chametz in its time in its kind and not in its kind in any amount like Rav — it implies certainly that Rav literally holds so; and although not for his reason, nevertheless in the substance of the law he holds like Rav; and now for Rav in its kind in any amount — for Rav also in dry, as Rabbeinu Tam wrote after retraction (see Tosafos Menachot 22 s.v. from here, etc.) (and what we learned that terumah in 101 is nullified in ordinary food that is fixed — see there); and Rav decreed not in its kind lest in its kind — the same applies in dry; Rava also holds thus. And although we have no ruling that the Mechaber would hold thus — in 447:64 and 461 and 453 in Magen Avraham 6: flour in flour is called wet in wet — therefore there is no proof from Beitzah 38b for R' Yehuda that wheat in wheat is not nullified — one may say it deals with flour; and see Tosafos Menachot and Yevamot chapter HaOrlah from this; and seemingly on the contrary there is contradiction to this from what the Mechaber wrote in Tur Choshen Mishpat general 45:6 and in Mechaber Choshen Mishpat 10 who gave a reason for doubt and did not give the Ran's reason brought in Taz Yoreh De'ah 102:5 and his praise because dry in dry in doubt should be nullified. Nevertheless I say the Mechaber from his own reasoning was stringent in chametz since he said in any amount like Rav and holds like Rabbeinu Tam and because of the stringency of chametz; and the halachah is like one view, and as we need to say below it will be explained; and see there he wrote dry in dry is not nullified in its kind specifically of doubt; and it is difficult — for the Rambam even not in its kind holds the reason in chametz because of doubt in chapter 13:13 in Magen Avraham; and Eshel Avraham Yoreh De'ah 102:14 — one may say here that one says dry is nullified in any amount and all hold chametz of doubt, and in its kind not like the Rambam, and not in its kind in wet in any amount because of karet and we do not dismiss; and one may say also the words of Or Sameach are thus; and it will be explained inside."

# 451 eliyah rabbah 5:_
T["451|eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt|5:_"] = "And if they filled, etc. This is the language of the Beit Yosef from the Rashba in Torat HaBayit; it seems he did not have the text in the Gemara \"and if it is full, gomeri, it is fine,\" for he wrote: and if in a vessel whose way is not to kindle from inside like a pot, even if they kindled from inside it is forbidden lest one attribute; and if he returned it to the furnace it is permitted — until here. And the same applies to a portable oven whose way is not to kindle from inside; and it is possible the Rashba agrees regarding the portable oven since it is made of bricks and clay — until here the language of the Beit Yosef. And in Chok Yaakov he extended to wonder why the Beit Yosef wondered on the Rashba — behold this distinction of the portable oven is proven at the end of the siman where it says if full gomeri it is permitted — see there. And it is not difficult, for since the Rashba did not bring the law of full gomeri there, it entered his mind that he did not have that text; and furthermore in the Gemara one may say that also in pots it is permitted when full gomeri as he wrote; and in Machilot Kevodo he saw only Torat HaBayit page 46, but it was omitted on page 33 that he ruled explicitly permitted when filled with coals; also in the long Torat HaBayit of the Rashba page 83 he did not see, where he copied the Gemara \"and if full gomeri it is fine\" and reads like our Gemara; also one may wonder on him, for it is clear explicitly to the Rashba there that he permits even in a pottery portable oven when full gomeri; and so implies Shulchan Aruch and Levush who wrote \"and so a small oven called padila,\" etc., and it is a pottery vessel as Beit Yosef wrote at the beginning of siman 461; and so I proved in my books to Yoreh De'ah beginning siman 97 from Tosafos Pesachim daf 30: if not so, what do they challenge on that portable oven that is permitted when full gomeri — behold the Torah testified about a pottery vessel that it never leaves its impurity — see there: behold the portable oven deals with bricks and clay; but perforce it is reasonable that it deals with pottery; and so explained the Rambam chapter 5 laws of chametz. Also I wondered from this on the Bach here who wrote in the name of Tosafos the opposite, that it deals with bricks and clay. And that which is established for us that kindling from outside does not help — one may say specifically pots, for he is concerned, which is not so in a portable oven that he is not concerned even of pottery; and so is explained in Rokeach end of siman 484; and all this I wrote briefly in Eliyahu Zuta. Also one may say that full gomeri that the Gemara permits applies also to pots: specifically for mere kindling we say he is concerned, which is not so when full gomeri — we see he is not concerned and it is like returning to the furnace; and so appears the view of Magen Avraham and so implies in Tosafos there; and so is explained explicitly in Pesakim Tosafot chapter Kol She'ah siman 88; and all this is not like Shibolei Leket and the tanna who explained full gomeri permitted — it deals specifically with an oven kindled from inside."

# 462 peri-megadim 1:א - fruit juice (abbreviated key points in existing file - expand)
T["462|peri-megadim/part-001.txt|1:א"] = "What the Mechaber wrote: fruit juice with water chametzes faster than other dough — it implies it is complete chametz according to Rambam; and in Tur it is written it becomes stiff; fruit juice with water — if it stood idle for the measure of a mil, at least; and so too Rosh z\"l Pesachim 35a (1) that stiff it is; and chametzed (Rambam); and as Tosafos wrote there s.v. fruit juice — and thus the language implies \"they are not liable for karet\" — but lashes according to Rambam; and although Tur in 442 wrote the conclusion of the Rosh is like the Rif that stiff is d'rabbanan — one must say two types of stiff — strained; and Peri Chadash here. And Rambam chapter 5 Choshen Mishpat 2 implies fruit juice with water is complete chametz literally and karet too one is liable; nevertheless there, dough kneaded in fruit juice — they are not liable for karet; likewise there is no prohibition in it and we do not establish it through water either — we learn from water karet too one is liable. And I thought to say the rejection of Magen Avraham from what he wrote in chapter 6 Choshen Mishpat likewise wine and milk and honey and olive oil eighteen — it is rich matzah; and other fruit juice goes out with water that is not rich matzah — behold without water olive oil does not come to chametz; and therefore with water according to Rambam it is forbidden, for if stiff still there is no eighteen — why do I need bread of affliction. But Rambam z\"l chapter 4 Choshen Mishpat 11 in garments washed in milk — it implies stiff for him is also a prohibition; nevertheless there that the form of chametz does not stand; nevertheless chapter 5 Choshen Mishpat mishnah of others implies red vinegar is complete chametz through mixtures; and he holds it chametzes in water with wine; and see Tosafos 35b s.v. fruit juice — it implies fruit juice with water karet too one is liable; and Peri Chadash. And therefore kneading dough water with fruit juice and doubt that it stood the measure of a mil — requires study, for one should not make a rabbinic doubt; and likewise for one who says stiff is nullified in sixty — Magen Avraham 447:5 requires study here, for it appears from Rambam and the sealed language of the Mechaber that he chametzed according to Rambam; and necessarily two types of stiff; but since there is no karet one may say nullified in sixty; and therefore ground matzah that they fried in fat and a wheat kernel was found inside — even though there is salt from our water — one may say it is stiff and nullified in sixty; and it will be explained below further on this. And see Menachot 54a: an apple they crushed, etc., and he concludes stiff it is; and for what Tosafos wrote fruit juice with water, etc. — we learn stiff it is and not karet; and Magen Avraham 1; and it will be explained there one may say it deals with a sour sharp apple and not water is stiff; and Peri Chadash."

